Responses from real authenticators, for t/24-Real-devices.t

fixtures.json holds the registrations and assertions that two other
WebAuthn libraries test with, recorded from real devices: Apple
(iOS, macOS and a passkey), Windows Hello and five other TPMs, two
Android phones, and YubiKeys and Security Keys from the NEO to the Bio.

  py_webauthn           https://github.com/duo-labs/py_webauthn
                        tests/test_verify_*_response*.py
                        at c9ba2b7dad6b76bec89e44c76c8ed81fc43287bb
  java-webauthn-server  https://github.com/Yubico/java-webauthn-server
                        webauthn-server-core/src/test/scala/com/yubico/
                        webauthn/test/RealExamples.scala
                        at 3a7edfb2609696808e8daad2508115bd43d88d3f

The responses were taken from those files as they are, re-encoded as
base64url. The post-quantum (ML-DSA) examples are left out, as this
module does not support ML-DSA, and so are the tests that build their
responses in code.

For each response the fixture records the result this module gives:
"expect" (accepted or refused), and the attestation type, the
signature count or the start of the error. Two differ from the source
library, each with a "note" saying why.

"time" is when the attestation's certificates are checked: the time
the source library's test sets, or else the day after the latest
notBefore in the certificate chain.

roots/ holds the published root certificates the chains end in:

  apple_webauthn_root_ca          Apple WebAuthn Root CA
  google_hardware_attestation_*   Google's Android key attestation roots
  microsoft_tpm_root_2014         Microsoft TPM Root Certificate
                                  Authority 2014
  yubico_u2f_root                 Yubico U2F Root CA Serial 457200631
  globalsign_root_ca              GlobalSign Root CA, which
                                  py_webauthn uses as a wrong root

The Apple, Google and GlobalSign roots are as py_webauthn ships them
(webauthn/helpers/known_root_certs.py), the Microsoft root as
java-webauthn-server's Windows Hello example gives it.

